// Every module the target-syntax ledger rows implicate, read from the ledger rather than restated, so
// a row added there widens this concern's relevance with no edit here.
data target_syntax_ledger_ref: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.target_syntax_transcription", decl_name: "target_syntax_debts")
data target_syntax_built_by_hand_in_application_modules: IntegrationConcern = IntegrationConcern {
  identity: "target_syntax_built_by_hand_in_application_modules",
  kind: SuspectedCapabilityGap {
    model: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.std.compilers.target_model", decl_name: "TargetValueExpressionProjection"),
    requirement: "an application written as ordinary .dag is emitted to Swift and TypeScript by the target model and each language's grammar rows, so no application module names a target language's syntax constructors (DESIGN section 4, one grammar read in both directions)",
    standing: RequirementEstablished { by: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.approve_ios_app", decl_name: "approve_ios_transcribed_swift_frontier") } },
  },
  affected: FreeSemigroup { head: target_syntax_ledger_ref, tail: flat_map(target_syntax_debts, d => debt_declarations(d: d)) },
  question: "Which forms must the shared value-expression projection and the declaration door gain -- and which browser and Node surfaces must be modelled as extdeps -- before each ledgered module can be written as ordinary .dag and emitted, and in what order do they unblock the most modules?",
  sightings: FreeSemigroup {
    head: ConcernSighting {
      revision: "fe796707f7a302c58830523b17b528c02c988f61",
      evidence: FreeSemigroup {
        head: CitesDeclaration { declaration: target_syntax_ledger_ref },
        tail: [
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.swift_expr_door", decl_name: "swift_syntax_forms_outside_value_expression_projection_frontier") },
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.approve_ios_app", decl_name: "approve_ios_bodied_member_frontier") },
        ],
      },
      account: "Read with git grep over dag/ and src/ for calls to any function named sw_* or ts_*, excluding the grammars (extdeps/languages and src/v2/extdeps/languages) and the compiler's emit and parse claims, and three name collisions that are not builders (v2.compiler.infer ts_declared_inhabitants_*, gunbc.instruments.emit_host_transport ts_host_stdout_is_the_sum, test.claim.scoped_authorization_scope_wire sw_scope). Swift: the five approve_ios_swift_* printers build every generated app file's tree; three of them are recorded by the app's own frontier rows, the two that print from a model (enrolment machine, operation errors) were recorded nowhere, and six app files are hand-written with retained rows. TypeScript: twelve browser and server client modules build their scripts as ts_* trees -- the largest are gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_component and gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_issue_sort, mostly ts_ident, ts_member, ts_call, ts_binop and string literals over DOM, fetch, timers and storage -- and none carried a dissolution or frontier row, so that debt was an untracked stall until the ledger. On the route side: the Swift door spells only identifiers, positional calls, member access, bool and int literals and memberwise init, and lists the rest at swift_syntax_forms_outside_value_expression_projection_frontier; the TypeScript route already emits closures, enum unions, imports and record construction from ordinary .dag by execution (src/v2/test/claim/execution typescript_*_by_execution), but has no row naming the forms it cannot, and no extdeps model of the browser surfaces the clients call. Not read: other targets (bash, YAML, CSS, HTML), and whether any client could migrate today.",
    },
    tail: [],
  },
  disposition: Triaged {
    home: ExistingHome { model: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.std.compilers.target_model", decl_name: "TargetValueExpressionProjection") },
    preserve: [
      PreservedObligation {
        holder: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.approve_ios_swift_wire", decl_name: "approve_ios_wire_swift_file"),
        control: Absent,
        statement: "a migrated module's emitted file stays what its consumer runs today: the app's Swift files and the served scripts change only where the migration intends, read against the files the printers produce at the revision the migration starts from",
      },
    ],
    next: climbs_when(capability: "the shared value-expression projection and the declaration door print the forms the ledgered modules use (statements, closures, string and collection literals, operators, switch bodies), and the browser and Node surfaces they call are modelled as extdeps, SUFFICIENT FOR one ledgered module per target -- a Swift model printer and a TypeScript client -- to be rewritten as ordinary .dag, emitted by the route, and its ledger row removed"),
  },
}

// THE CAS GENERATION RULE IS DECIDED TWICE. Whether an attempt's expectation is admissible against what
// a compare-and-set slot holds is answered by std.durable_compare_and_set's cas_expectation_admits AND,
// independently, by the ProbedAbsent arm of gunbc.durable_cas_file_store's cas_compare_and_set_outcome.
// The fork is measured, not inferred: a mutation that makes the std arm admit ExpectSlotGeneration against
// an absent observation leaves EVERY row of test.claim.durable_cas_file_store_wet_witness green (branch
// sharp-otter-429/d6-witness-main-mutant-stdfold at 2167708a09307e9c24dc0718401e02570d4b690f, six rows
// PASS), while the same retired ordering restored in the store's own arm turns exactly the empty-store row
// red and leaves the other five green (505c4b6e2839beefe999a13cea3502b1d7822df2). A mutation of the std
// rule cannot reach the store's rows at all, which is what a duplicated rule looks like in execution. The
// std fold's only consumer is gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier's roadmap_event_parent_admitted.
data cas_generation_expectation_admitted_twice: IntegrationConcern = IntegrationConcern {
  identity: "cas_generation_expectation_admitted_twice",
  kind: SuspectedFork {
    authorities: DistinctAuthorities {
      first: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.durable_cas_file_store", decl_name: "cas_compare_and_set_outcome"),
      second: decl_ref(module_path: "std.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "cas_expectation_admits"),
      rest: [],
    },
  },
  affected: FreeSemigroup {
    head: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.durable_cas_file_store", decl_name: "cas_compare_and_set_outcome"),
    tail: [
      decl_ref(module_path: "std.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "cas_expectation_admits"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier", decl_name: "roadmap_event_parent_admitted"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_cas_file_store_wet_witness", decl_name: "a_generation_expectation_against_an_empty_store_refuses_and_writes_no_slot_by_real_execution"),
    ],
  },
  question: "Which declaration decides whether an attempt's generation expectation is admissible against what a compare-and-set slot holds, and in particular who refuses ExpectSlotGeneration against a slot that was never created?",
  sightings: FreeSemigroup {
    head: ConcernSighting {
      revision: d6_witness_measurement_revision,
      evidence: FreeSemigroup {
        head: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.durable_cas_file_store", decl_name: "cas_compare_and_set_outcome") },
        tail: [
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "std.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "cas_expectation_admits") },
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_cas_file_store_wet_witness", decl_name: "a_generation_expectation_against_an_empty_store_refuses_and_writes_no_slot_by_real_execution") },
        ],
      },
      account: "Six rows of the store's wet witness were run at two heads. Mutating the std fold's absent arm to admit a generation expectation: six green, no row moved. Mutating the store's own ProbedAbsent arm to commit a generation expectation at the successor of the EXPECTED generation -- the ordering the store's header retires -- reddened exactly the empty-store row. The store reads the slot and decides inline; it never calls the std fold. So the rule the store enforces and the rule the std fold enforces are two rules, kept in agreement by nothing.",
    },
    tail: [],
  },
  disposition: Triaged {
    home: ExistingHome { model: decl_ref(module_path: "std.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "cas_expectation_admits") },
    preserve: [
      PreservedObligation {
        holder: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.durable_cas_file_store", decl_name: "cas_compare_and_set_outcome"),
        control: decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_cas_file_store_wet_witness", decl_name: "a_generation_expectation_against_an_empty_store_refuses_and_writes_no_slot_by_real_execution"),
        statement: "an ExpectSlotGeneration attempt against a store holding nothing refuses, the refusal names the absence rather than a competing version, and the store is left with no slot file at all",
      },
    ],
    next: climbs_when(capability: "the file store's absent-slot arm consumes std.durable_compare_and_set's cas_expectation_admits and its inline copy is deleted, so a mutation of that one rule reddens a store row -- measured today, a mutation of that rule reddens none"),
  },
}

// THE FOLD'S OWN CLAIMS HAVE NO EXECUTING WITNESS. test.claim.durable_compare_and_set states the fold's
// decision table, including the superseded-generation refusal the store relies on, but its module resolves
// none of its own types: it imports neither std.types (which declares NonEmptyStr) nor
// std.durable_compare_and_set. Measured at base bbec474015b with the same compiler used for the runs beside
// the row above: the entry refuses with 134 hard errors, the first an unresolved type 'LiveTreeDisposition'
// at the file's first data declaration, and the same 134 with src/v1 added as a source root. The claim lane
// discovers rows by the test fn marker in the file and runs them by name, so an entry that never resolves
// has zero executable rows: its nine rows are unevaluated claims, and they have been silently rotting. A row
// of mine added there was reverted for exactly this reason -- it could not go red.
data cas_fold_claims_have_no_executing_witness: IntegrationConcern = IntegrationConcern {
  identity: "cas_fold_claims_have_no_executing_witness",
  kind: SuspectedCapabilityGap {
    model: decl_ref(module_path: "std.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "cas_expectation_admits"),
    requirement: "a generation-expectation rule that the store enforces has a witness row that executes at the entry it is claimed from, so a change to the rule cannot land without a row going red",
    standing: RequirementEstablished {
      by: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_cas_file_store_wet_witness", decl_name: "a_generation_expectation_against_an_empty_store_refuses_and_writes_no_slot_by_real_execution") },
    },
  },
  affected: FreeSemigroup {
    head: decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "an_update_from_a_superseded_generation_refuses_and_reports_the_current_head"),
    tail: [
      decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "an_attempt_against_the_last_generation_refuses_as_exhausted_and_one_below_commits_to_it"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "std.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "cas_expectation_admits"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_cas_file_store_wet_witness", decl_name: "a_generation_expectation_against_an_empty_store_refuses_and_writes_no_slot_by_real_execution"),
    ],
  },
  question: "Does any claim in test.claim.durable_compare_and_set lack a witness elsewhere -- and if none does, is that entry revived (given its imports, its rows re-checked against the current fold) or retired with each claim named against the row that now covers it?",
  sightings: FreeSemigroup {
    head: ConcernSighting {
      revision: d6_witness_base_revision,
      evidence: FreeSemigroup {
        head: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "an_update_from_a_superseded_generation_refuses_and_reports_the_current_head") },
        tail: [CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "std.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "cas_generation_exhausted") }],
      },
      account: "Compiling the file as its own entry produced 134 hard errors, the first an unresolved type at the file's first data declaration; adding src/v1 as a source root changed nothing. The row I had added there was reverted rather than left as an unexecutable claim. The same rule is witnessed today only at the store surface, by the row named in the requirement above.",
    },
    tail: [],
  },
  disposition: Triaged {
    home: HomeUnresolved {
      question: "revive the entry beside std.durable_compare_and_set's own claims, or retire it and name where each of its nine claims is witnessed",
    },
    preserve: [
      PreservedObligation {
        holder: decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.durable_compare_and_set", decl_name: "an_update_from_a_superseded_generation_refuses_and_reports_the_current_head"),
        control: Absent,
        statement: "an update attempted from a generation the store no longer holds refuses and reports the generation the store does hold; wherever that claim is witnessed, it must go red if the refusal stops naming the current head",
      },
    ],
    next: climbs_when(capability: "the entry compiles at its own entry and its rows execute, or the file is retired with every claim named against the row that covers it"),
  },
}

// THE SERVED-OBSERVATION WRITE IS HAND-ROLLED BESIDE THE STORE LIBRARY. `served_observation_index_put` and
// `served_observation_body_put` each issue a plain `Filesystem.Write` into the instance's `served-observations`
// directory (the body first, the index last, so a reader never sees an index naming a body that is not there --
// the module's own header says so), while every other durable record this lane sealed is put through
// `gunbc.durable_cas_file_store`'s committed generations. One question -- who may put bytes where a record
// stands, and what happens when two writers try -- is answered twice, and the two answers are kept in agreement
// by nothing: the library refuses a lost race, the hand route cannot see one. Recorded here rather than closed:
// the PR that sealed this store's census left both writes in place, so its finding row is narrowed to them and
